      "title": {
        "zh": "解构顾客满意度的配方：旅游情境下利用在线评论力量的动态组态框架",
        "en": "Deconstructing customer satisfaction recipes: A dynamic configurational framework leveraging the power of online reviews in tourism contexts"
      },
      "summary": {
        "zh": "<p>著录（据出版商正式版 PDF，Tourism Management 110 (2025) 105181）：</p>\n<p>Qin, Y., Luo, C. G., &amp; Ngai, E. W. T. (2025). Deconstructing customer satisfaction recipes: A dynamic configurational framework leveraging the power of online reviews in tourism contexts. Tourism Management, 110, 105181. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tourman.2025.105181</p>\n<p>摘要（译自原文）：</p>\n<p>在线评论环境中，产品或服务属性表现与顾客满意度之间的复杂关系已被广泛讨论。然而，在整体思维下——尤其是考虑属性间的时间交互时——何种属性组态能够提升满意度，仍缺乏研究。为弥补这一缺口，本研究基于复杂性与三因素理论，构建一个动态组态框架，用以解构旅游情境下提升顾客满意度的属性配方及其时间演化。通过直接从游客评论中提取关键服务属性与情感，研究识别出随时间持续达成高满意度的模式。这是首个从顾客体验视角将在线评论挖掘与面板模糊集定性比较分析相结合的研究。研究揭示存在多条等价的因果路径，不同游客群体的满意度路径各异。实践上，规范性因果配方有助于决策者优化旅游资源配置，并提供战略洞见以动态响应游客需求。</p>\n<p>关键词：顾客满意度；在线评论；面板 fsQCA；三因素理论；旅游情境</p>",
        "en": "<p>Bibliographic record (from the publisher's final PDF, Tourism Management 110 (2025) 105181):</p>\n<p>Qin, Y., Luo, C. G., &amp; Ngai, E. W. T. (2025). Deconstructing customer satisfaction recipes: A dynamic configurational framework leveraging the power of online reviews in tourism contexts. Tourism Management, 110, 105181. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tourman.2025.105181</p>\n<p>Abstract:</p>\n<p>The complex relationship between product or service attribute performance and customer satisfaction in online review environments has been widely discussed. However, attribute configuration for enhancing customer satisfaction under holistic thinking, particularly considering temporal interactions among attributes, remains underexplored. To address this gap, this study develops a dynamic configurational framework based on complexity and three-factor theories to deconstruct attribute recipes and their temporal evolution for improving customer satisfaction in tourism contexts. By extracting key service attributes and affects directly from tourist reviews, it identifies patterns that consistently achieve high satisfaction over time. This is the first study to integrate online review mining with panel fuzzy-set qualitative comparative analysis from a customer experience perspective. Findings reveal the existence of multiple equivalent causal pathways, with distinct satisfaction pathways for different tourist segments. Practically, normative causal recipes assist policymakers in optimizing tourism resource allocation and providing strategic insights to dynamically respond to tourist demands.</p>\n<p>Keywords: Customer satisfaction; Online reviews; Panel fsQCA; Three-factor theory; Tourism contexts</p>"
      }
